::CW_U01_L01_GP_Q01::[MLA.CW.COM.02] A peer says, "Your narrator's repeated question helped me feel the character's worry. You might add one action after the question so the worry becomes visible." Which statement best describes this feedback?{
~A. It is weak because all feedback should only praise the writer.#Incorrect. Workshop feedback can include praise and revision guidance as long as it stays specific and respectful.
~B. It is weak because it tells the writer to change the entire story.#Incorrect. The comment gives one focused suggestion, not a demand to rewrite everything.
=C. It is useful because it names a craft choice, explains the effect, and gives a revision suggestion.#Correct. The feedback identifies repetition, explains the reader effect, and offers a focused next move.
~D. It is useful because it judges the writer's talent.#Incorrect. Strong workshop feedback focuses on the writing, not the writer's personal talent.
}

::CW_U01_L01_GP_Q02::[MLA.CW.COM.02] Which workshop comment is most helpful for revision?{
~A. This was nice and I liked all of it.#Incorrect. This is friendly but too general to guide revision.
=B. The opening image is clear, but the setting could use one sound detail to help the reader enter the scene.#Correct. This names a strength, identifies a specific area, and gives a usable next step.
~C. You should make it better before anyone reads it.#Incorrect. This is disrespectful and gives no specific craft direction.
~D. I would write a different story because this one is not my style.#Incorrect. This shifts control away from the writer and does not respond to the draft's craft choices.
}

::CW_U01_L01_GP_Q03::[MLA.CW.COM.04] In a workshop, a student is learning academic language for creative writing. Which sentence frame best supports access to craft discussion?{
~A. I do not understand this, so it is wrong.#Incorrect. This shuts down discussion and does not use academic craft language.
~B. Everyone should already know what I mean.#Incorrect. This does not support clear academic communication.
~C. The story is good because it is good.#Incorrect. This repeats an opinion without craft vocabulary.
=D. I noticed the writer's use of ___, and it creates ___ for the reader.#Correct. This frame helps the student name a craft choice and explain its effect.
}

::CW_U01_L01_GP_Q04::[MLA.CW.COM.05] Which action shows active listening during a Creative Writing workshop?{
=A. Taking notes on specific words, images, or moments you may discuss after the writer finishes.#Correct. This shows attention and prepares specific feedback.
~B. Planning your own response while the writer is still reading.#Incorrect. Active listening requires attention to the speaker's words before preparing your response.
~C. Interrupting to correct every sentence immediately.#Incorrect. Interrupting prevents the writer from sharing the piece fully.
~D. Looking away and waiting for the quiz to begin.#Incorrect. This does not show engagement with the writer or the discussion.
}

::CW_U01_L01_GP_Q05::[MLA.CW.COM.02] A writer asks for feedback on whether the ending feels complete. Which response best matches the writer's request?{
~A. I liked the beginning more than the ending.#Incorrect. This gives a preference but does not explain whether the ending feels complete.
~B. Your handwriting is neat in the final paragraph.#Incorrect. This does not address the writer's question about the ending.
=C. The ending resolves the argument, but the character's final feeling is unclear. You might add one sentence showing what the character notices after the argument ends.#Correct. This addresses completion, identifies a gap, and suggests a focused revision.
~D. You should change the main character's name.#Incorrect. This ignores the requested focus. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}
